# The agent layer as observable behavior
#
# This is what actually matters. The framework is an implementation detail of
# these responses. If your stack produces them, the layer is correct, whatever
# it is written in.
#
# Format: raw HTTP. Paste into an .http client, or read as a specification.

### 1. The markdown twin exists at the page URL plus .md

GET https://example.com/guides/onboarding.md

# EXPECTED
# HTTP/1.1 200 OK
# Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8
# Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, s-maxage=3600
# X-Robots-Tag: all
# Link: <https://example.com/guides/onboarding>; rel="canonical"
#
# ---
# title: Onboarding guide
# source_url: https://example.com/guides/onboarding
# updated: 2026-05-14
# ---
#
# # Onboarding guide
# ...
#
# NOTES
# - The canonical MUST come from the document, not from the request path.
# - X-Robots-Tag: all is deliberate. noindex here would also stop AI fetchers
#   that respect it, which defeats the purpose of publishing the twin.


### 2. A missing page is a 404, never an empty 200

GET https://example.com/guides/does-not-exist.md

# EXPECTED
# HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
# Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
#
# 404 - not found
#
# An empty 200 is worse than a 404: it teaches an agent the page exists and is
# blank, and it enters indexes as a real page.


### 3. The home twin lives at /index.md and canonicals to the root

GET https://example.com/index.md

# EXPECTED
# Link: <https://example.com/>; rel="canonical"
#
# Deriving the canonical from the path yields /index, which 404s. Pass the root
# explicitly for this one page.


### 4. The HTML page points at its twin

GET https://example.com/guides/onboarding

# EXPECTED (in the head)
# <link rel="canonical" href="https://example.com/guides/onboarding">
# <link rel="alternate" type="text/markdown" href="https://example.com/guides/onboarding.md">


### 5. The map for models

GET https://example.com/llms.txt

# EXPECTED
# Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
#
# # Site Name
#
# > One sentence about what this site is for.
#
# Markdown for agents: pages in the sections below also answer as clean markdown
# by adding `.md` to the URL.
#
# ## Questions we answer
# - How do I do X?: https://example.com/guides/x.md
# ...
#
# RULE: every URL this file advertises must return 200. A promised convention
# that 404s costs the trust of the whole file.


### 6. The whole corpus, for bulk ingestion

GET https://example.com/llms-full.txt

# EXPECTED
# Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
# One file, every published page, generated from the same functions that render
# the individual twins.


### 7. Crawl rules

GET https://example.com/robots.txt

# EXPECTED
# User-agent: *
# Disallow: /api/
# Disallow: /dashboard/
# Allow: /
# Allow: /api/og            <- more specific than the /api/ block
#
# User-agent: GPTBot
# User-agent: ChatGPT-User
# User-agent: ClaudeBot
# User-agent: PerplexityBot
# Allow: /
#
# Sitemap: https://example.com/sitemap.xml


### 8. The sitemap lists HTML only

GET https://example.com/sitemap.xml

# EXPECTED
# - No URL ending in .md. Listing both renderings asks to be assessed for
#   duplicate content.
# - <lastmod> present only where a real content date backs it.
# - Every listed URL returns 200 and canonicals to itself.


### 9. A clean arrival sets no cookie

GET https://example.com/

# EXPECTED
# No Set-Cookie header. Writing a cookie on every visit invalidates the CDN
# cache for the highest-traffic pages on the site.


### 10. An arrival with a signal does set one

GET https://example.com/
Referer: https://chatgpt.com/

# EXPECTED
# Set-Cookie: first_touch=...; Max-Age=7776000; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Secure


### Verification one-liners
#
#   curl -sI https://example.com/guides/onboarding.md
#   curl -s  https://example.com/guides/nope.md | head -1
#   curl -s  https://example.com/llms.txt | grep -oE "https://[^ )]+\.md" | sort -u \
#     | while read u; do echo "$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' "$u") $u"; done
#   curl -s  https://example.com/sitemap.xml | grep -c "\.md</loc>"    # expect 0
#   curl -sI https://example.com/ | grep -i set-cookie                 # expect nothing
#
# Run these against a production build. A dev server hides caching and bundling
# failures that only appear once deployed.
